The Full Decentralized Commons Treaty Plan
Phase 1: Identify the Pillars of the Commons
The first step is to rally a core coalition of organizations that are already working on decentralized infrastructure. These are your "anchor tenants."

The Core Tech & Archive Alliance
The most crucial partnership to solidify is between the big decentralized storage networks and the institutions that hold our digital memory.

The Internet Archive is arguably the central node for this effort. Its founder, Brewster Kahle, has been a vocal advocate for a decentralized web for years, framing it as a way to build a "robust and private Internet that has a history that will persist over decades and maybe centuries."

This vision has already attracted major funding from decentralized tech, like the Filecoin Foundation's 50,000 FIL grant to the Internet Archive, one of the largest donations in the Archive's history. This money is specifically earmarked to make the Archive "more decentralized," showing a direct alignment of interests.

Bringing in the Standard-Setters
The tech needs to be standardized to work together. This is where formal bodies come in.

The IRTF's DINRG (Decentralized Internet Infrastructure Research Group) is an official research group chartered to investigate open issues in decentralizing infrastructure like identity, naming, and storage.

This group is exactly the forum you are describing. Its stated goals include investigating use cases, assessing solutions for deployment, and developing best practices. Importantly, they are aware that they need to "reach other communities and organizations," specifically mentioning bodies like the ITU and IEEE.

Phase 2: Establish the "Common Ground"
This is where you move from talking to agreeing on a shared technical and governance framework.

A Charter that Starts with the Hard Truth
The treaty's foundation should not be blind idealism. The IETF's DINRG has pointed out a crucial insight: decentralized discussions often focus only on the pros, but it is better to start with the cons. The charter should be an honest assessment of the challenges—scalability, trust management, and economic incentives—that all projects face. It would also be grounded in the reality that decentralization is "an unfolding process," not a destination.

A Technical Stack for the Commons
Based on the existing work, the treaty would likely commit to:

A Shared Naming and Lookup System: This is a primary focus of DINRG's research.

Interoperable Decentralized Storage: Committing to common standards to avoid fragmentation, building on the collaboration already happening between the Archive and projects like Filecoin, IPFS, and GUN.

Economic Incentive Primitives: This is a "recurrent artifact" (recurring issue) in these discussions. A successful commons needs a shared understanding of how to reward node operators and participants, avoiding the "every project reinvents the wheel" problem.

Governance Model: The Steward as Community Node
The "steward" is a role, not a legal entity:

Rotating—no permanent power holders

Transparent—all decisions public, all meetings recorded

Accountable—if the steward acts badly, the community forks them out

Interest-neutral—the steward has no financial stake in any protocol

Voting: One person, one vote. Not token-weighted. Not corporate-weighted. Big players get the same say as solo developers.

Enforcement: Not legal. Social. If you violate the treaty, the community ignores your implementation. You become incompatible. You lose relevance.

Phase 3: Build a Self-Sustaining System
The final phase is to make the treaty a living, working reality.

dweb.archive.org: Your Proof of Concept
This is not a theoretical proposal. The Internet Archive has already built a working prototype: dweb.archive.org.

This decentralized version of Archive.org uses a combination of peer-to-peer protocols like IPFS, WebTorrent, and GUN to deliver content.

It serves as a "minimum viable product" and the most compelling thing to show any potential stakeholder. It proves the concept works, even if it's "a bit slower" than the centralized version—a clear area for the treaty to address.

The "What's in it for Me?" Pitch
When you talk to people and organizations, your message should be:

To Tech Builders: "Join a formal, neutral forum (like DINRG) to help define the standards that will make your project part of a larger, more resilient web, rather than a silo."

To Archivists and Librarians: "You are key to this. Help us build a 'networked' solution that guarantees the integrity of information beyond the control of any single entity."

To Funders and Foundations: "Invest in the infrastructure of this new web, not just individual projects. By funding the treaty process itself, you are funding the foundational layer for a more open and resilient internet."

Phase 4: Gather Intel and Interest (Parallel Process)
Think of your initial detective work as a parallel process:

Institutional path: The IRTF's DINRG is your ideal forum. It's already an active, open platform for discussing causes, mitigation solutions, and coordinating efforts. Formal talks here require less upfront negotiation.

Community path: Relationships are already seeded. The Internet Archive and Filecoin have a multi-year partnership and share the goal of preserving the web's history. The DWeb community has been holding global summits and camps since 2016, proving these conversations are not new.

Estimated time: 6–12 months. You don't need to start from scratch.

Phase 5: Get the "Top Players" in the Room
This is where you move from gathering to convening.

Formal track: For a standards body like the ISO, the recommended timeframe to develop a standard is 36 months from proposal to publication. DINRG is a research group, but expect a similar cadence for serious, binding agreements.

Community track: The model here is the 2016 Decentralized Web Summit, where 350 pioneers from tech, academia, and civil society gathered. A serious "summit" of top players could be organized as a dedicated working meeting.

Estimated time: 1–2 years using existing forums; 2–3 years for a standalone summit.

Phase 6: Investigate Needs and Plan Future Phases
The post-summit phase is where you produce tangible outputs.

A critical pre-step: Develop a common terminology and foster a better understanding of the constraints of different technologies.

Planning future phases: Move from diagnosis (root causes) to treatment (mitigation solutions). Document outcomes (research papers, RFCs) and interact with the broader community.

Estimated time: 2–5 years to go from high-level charter to documented standards.

Phase 7: Draft and Ratify the Treaty
The treaty itself is a living document:

Draft the charter in plain English. No legalese.

Publish it publicly on GitHub, IPFS, Archive.org.

Open comment period for 6–12 months.

Revise based on feedback.

Ratify through community vote (rough consensus, not unanimity).

Implement reference implementations.

Iterate—the treaty is never "finished."

Phase 8: Build the Steward Entity
Create a minimal legal entity only if needed (to hold funds, sign contracts). Otherwise, keep it as a social contract enforced by reputation and interoperability.

The steward's only job:

Convene meetings

Maintain the charter

Facilitate dispute resolution

Report transparently on all activities

No power. Just coordination.

