The Current Landscape
Formal Standards & Research: The DINRG
The most direct match. It's an official, active research group under the IRTF. The group is formally chartered to investigate the root causes of internet centralization, develop common terminology, and facilitate discussions between researchers and organizations.

Key point: The goal is to create a common understanding and a forum for large-scale coordination—the "common ground" you identified as essential.

Summits & Community Collaboration: The Decentralized Web Summit
In 2016, the Internet Archive hosted the first Decentralized Web Summit, bringing together over 300 "web architects, activists, archivists, and policy makers." Internet pioneers like Tim Berners-Lee and Vint Cerf attended.

Tim Berners-Lee summarized the core challenge: "The web is already decentralized... The problem is the dominance of one search engine, one big social network... We don't have a technology problem, we have a social problem."

Building the Commons: Decentralized Storage & Archiving
The most concrete is the partnership between the Internet Archive and the Filecoin Foundation to preserve public datasets on decentralized storage networks.

The government of Bermuda is currently uploading public records to this network through the Democracy's Library project to ensure they are "permanently accessible, tamper-resistant, and verifiable."

The Status: An Ongoing, Slow-Moving Process
DINRG has spent years examining the problem, but at a recent meeting, the chairs noted that discussions have "stayed at a high level, without identifying specific actionable steps."

The Decentralized Web Summit was held in 2016, but there is no indication it became a recurring, formal "treaty negotiation" event.

The gap: The process is fragmented—research, storage infrastructure, and summits exist separately. No one has unified them into a single treaty effort.

Why No One Has Unified It Yet
Reason	Explanation
Ideological Purity	"Taking command" is seen as centralization, even if it's coordination.
Competing Incentives	Crypto projects want their token to win. Academics want their paper cited. Corporations want their edge.
NIH Syndrome	Builders love their own architecture. Admitting someone else's is better feels like surrender.
Funding Fragmentation	Grants are project-specific. No one funds coordination—it's not sexy.
Fear of Capture	Even a neutral convener will be attacked as "the new centralizer."
The Fix: A Social Contract, Not a Legal One
You don't need lawyers. You need trust.

Legal Binding	Social Contract (Your Model)
Enforced by courts	Enforced by reputation and community shunning
Slow, expensive	Fast, cooperative
Requires jurisdiction	No jurisdiction—global by design
Can be gamed by lawyers	Can only be gamed by losing trust
The real enforcement: If you ignore the treaty, the community ignores you. Your project becomes irrelevant.

